---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

// Added 27 September 2026 (XC) so the modality section shows analysis, human study and model study
// together. Diagnostic runs on pretrained models exist but are not calibrated; controlled training
// has not started. Report no effect sizes here, and keep it separate from the thesis.
const project = projects.modalModels;
---

<Base
  title={`${project.title} · Xiaochuan Li`}
  description="A developing project connecting simple accounts of modal reasoning with language-model behaviour and planned internal mechanism tests."
  showProgress={true}
  toc={true}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="!mb-6 !text-sm"><a href="/research#current" class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">← Current Research: Modality</a></p>
    <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-dim)] font-medium mb-2">{project.status}</div>
    <h1>{project.title}</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] italic !text-lg !mb-3">{project.question}</p>
    <p>{project.summary} {project.connection}</p>

    <h2>From a linguistic contrast to a cognitive model</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/research/three-ways">The linguistic analysis</a> supplies distinctions about
      possibility, knowledge, and conversational commitments. The proposed next step is to
      state a small computational account: which alternatives it represents, how evidence
      updates them, and what responses it predicts. Its cognitive interpretation must be tested
      against independent human evidence, including suitable developmental findings.
    </p>

    <h2>What already exists</h2>
    <p>
      Diagnostic runs on pretrained models tested how a possibility is treated after exclusion
      and attempted reopening, using matched continuations and instrument checks. Results are
      mixed and independent material calibration is incomplete. These runs motivate the next
      question; they do not establish a cognitive model or an internal mechanism.
    </p>

    <h2>Testing a proposed computation inside an LM</h2>
    <p>
      Once a candidate model makes clear predictions, the planned comparison asks whether its
      intermediate variables can be aligned with LM representations. Changing an aligned state
      should produce the specific changes predicted by the small model, including answers that
      should remain unchanged. Tests on new examples and competing models are needed to separate
      an explanatory correspondence from a convenient readout of the answer.
    </p>

    <h2>Learning from controlled input</h2>
    <p>
      Controlled training remains a complementary way to investigate which input cues or
      learning assumptions support the target distinction. It is a different question from
      locating an existing computation in a fixed pretrained model. A model trained to follow
      a prescribed cognitive account cannot then be used as evidence that the original LM
      acquired that account spontaneously.
    </p>

    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p class="cw-status">
      Diagnostic runs exist. The cognitive account is being specified; controlled training and
      causal alignment tests have not begun. This project is separate from the human master's thesis.
    </p>
    <p class="!mt-10 !text-sm !text-[var(--color-text-dim)]"><a href="/research">← Back to Research & Projects</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .cw-status { font-size: 0.98rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-text); padding: 1rem 1.2rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent); background: var(--color-surface); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0; }
</style>
